.. rubric:: Port and outputs

``EigenmodePort`` / ``#eigenmode_port`` defines both the modal basis and
monitor; ``EigenmodeExcitation`` selects what is driven. Each numbered port
writes ``/eigenmode_ports/portN`` (under ``/subgrids/<subgrid ID>`` for an
owning HSG grid). ``frequency``, ``incident`` and ``outgoing`` are modal
spectra, not lumped voltage/current histories. Follow ``ModeIndices`` and
the stored power matrices; do not assign an arbitrary 50 Ohm circuit reference.

With one active channel, per-port ``S`` arrays describe responses to that
channel. Multiple simultaneous drives produce active reflection data, not a
complete S matrix; fully passive virtual ports produce raw spectra without S.
Use ``coefficient_valid_S`` for coefficient inspection and
``power_wave_valid_S`` for propagating-power interpretation. Below-cutoff
coefficients must not be interpreted as transmitted power.

Use the modal plots and post-processing examples in :doc:`eigenmode_port`,
not the conventional ``plot_port`` tool. See :ref:`eigenmode-port-output`
for shapes and files and :ref:`study-eigenmode` for complete matrices and
array synthesis. Ports must satisfy the owning grid/backend restrictions in
the eigenmode guide.

Ports using automatic tracking also write a versioned ``mode_tracking`` group.
It contains original candidate mappings, overlaps, residuals, requested and
adaptive frequencies, and per-anchor confinement/artifact diagnostics. These
quality records do not alter the established ``anchor_mode_valid`` or power-wave
masks; they explain warnings about anchors that remain in use.
